<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B76/current/B7600024/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:B76::B7600024";
        dc:date                "2020-10-27 12:25:06.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:B76::B7600024";
        pav:authoredOn         "2020-10-27 12:25:06.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B76/current/B7600024/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "UoW Seaglider M1";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L06/current/27/>;
        skos:definition        "An autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV) based on buoyancy, developed at the University of Washington. It was developed for continuous, long-term measurement of oceanographic parameters. It uses small changes in buoyancy and wings to achieve forward motion. The systems pitch and roll are controlled using adjustable ballast (the vehicle battery). The vehicle moves through the water in a saw-tooth like pattern and surfaces often to determine its position. Navigation is accomplished using a combination of GPS fixes while on the surface and internal sensors that monitor the vehicle heading, depth and attitude during dives. External sensors are constantly scanning the ocean to determine environmental properties. The glider is 1.8 - 2 m long with a maximum diameter of 30 cm and antenna mast length between 43 cm and 1 m. It weighs 52 kg with a wing span of 1 m. It has a deployment range of 4600 km, deployment length of 10 months and an operating depth range between 50-1000 m. Maximum speed is 25 cm/s."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:B76::B7600024";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "University of Washington Seaglider M1 glider"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L35/current/MAN0024/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B75/current/ORG00142/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B76/current/B7600043/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:B76::B7600043";
        dc:date                "2025-11-18 10:02:46.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:B76::B7600043";
        pav:authoredOn         "2025-11-18 10:02:46.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B76/current/B7600043/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "RAFOS float";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L06/current/44/>;
        skos:definition        "A drifting subsurface float used to map deep ocean currents and deployed at a target range of depths known as the Sound Fixing and Ranging (SOFAR) channel. The RAFOS float acts as a receiver of pongs emitted at designated times by moored acoustic beacons at ranges of hundreds of kilometres away which provide real-time underwater tracking of the float. Widely used by Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution (WHOI), the floats comprise a 2 m glass tube to house the electronics, an acoustic hydrophone, a ballast weight, and is typically fitted with temperature, pressure, and dissolved oxygen sensors. The float is depth rated to 3000 m and can have a deployment life span of up to 2 years. At the end of its mission, a pre-programmed command from the float's microprocessor releases the ballast weight, allowing it to rise to the surface and transmit the acoustic tracking data to satellites. The RAFOS system for oceanographic current measurements was first developed by T. Rossby, D. Dorson, and J. Fontaine at the University of Rhode Island and published in 1986. Rossby, T., Dorson, D., and Fontaine, J. (1986) The RAFOS System. J. Atmos. Oceanic Technol., 3(4), 672–679. doi: 10.1175/1520-0426(1986)0032.0.CO;2."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:B76::B7600043";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "Unknown RAFOS drifting subsurface float - Rossby et al. (1986)"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B75/current/UNSPEC/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L35/current/MAN0002/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B76/current/B7600035/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:B76::B7600035";
        dc:date                "2025-04-09 17:02:41.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:B76::B7600035";
        pav:authoredOn         "2025-04-09 17:02:41.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B76/current/B7600035/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "Alseamar SeaExplorer X2";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L06/current/27/>;
        skos:definition        "A long-range autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV) based on buoyancy. It is used for remote water column sampling. It uses hydraulic buoyancy change to alter the vehicle density in relation to the surrounding water thereby causing the vehicle to either float or sink. Given an appropriate dive or climb angle, the wings and body lift and convert some of this vertical motion into a forward saw tooth horizontal motion. Periodically, the glider surfaces and calls via Iridium Satellite Phone (anywhere in world) or Free Wave RF Modem (line of sight) in to landstation name GLIMPS 0auto attendant computer) to relay navigational fix, data and receive further instructions for command and control. The glider is capable of storm sampling and can be flown in a coordinated fleet. It is about 2 m in length (+0.8 m foldable antenna), has a hull diameter of about 23 cm and mass of 60 kg in the air. Wing span 65 cm. Ballast 1000 cc, 20 to 1000 m depth range of operation. Rechargeable battery. It transmits via RF modem, Iridium (RUDICS), ARGOS or acoustic modem."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:B76::B7600035";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "Alseamar SeaExplorer X2 glider"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L35/current/MAN0328/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B75/current/ORG02100/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B76/current/B7600037/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:B76::B7600037";
        dc:date                "2025-04-09 17:02:41.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:B76::B7600037";
        pav:authoredOn         "2025-04-09 17:02:41.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B76/current/B7600037/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "Alseamar SeaExplorer X3 shallow";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L06/current/27/>;
        skos:definition        "A long-range autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV) based on buoyancy, released in 2025 with updated electronics compared to the X2. It has a specific pump and ballast capacity to optimise flight in the shallow 5 to 200 m depth range. It is used for remote water column sampling. It uses hydraulic buoyancy change to alter the vehicle density in relation to the surrounding water thereby causing the vehicle to either float or sink. Given an appropriate dive or climb angle, the wings and body lift and convert some of this vertical motion into a forward saw tooth horizontal motion. Periodically, the glider surfaces and calls via Iridium Satellite Phone (anywhere in world) or Free Wave RF Modem (line of sight) in to landstation name GLIMPS 0auto attendant computer) to relay navigational fix, data and receive further instructions for command and control. The glider is capable of storm sampling and can be flown in a coordinated fleet. It is about 2.2 m in length (+0.8 m foldable antenna), has a hull diameter of about 23 cm and mass of 65 kg in the air. Wing span 65 cm. Ballast 1600 cc, 5m to 200 m depth range of operation. Rechargeable battery. It transmits via RF modem, Iridium (RUDICS), ARGOS or acoustic modem."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:B76::B7600037";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "Alseamar SeaExplorer X3 shallow glider"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/L35/current/MAN0328/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B75/current/ORG02100/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/B76/current/B7600036/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
